    },
    "grade": "11-12",
    "points": 4,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "In the square $ABCD$ of side 1 we join $A$ and $D$ by a zigzag line touching the side $BC$ twice and $AD$ once (see the picture). What is the shortest path length?",
    "image": "qimages/kangaroo/ca-2019-g1112/q14.webp",
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$3\\sqrt2$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$2\\sqrt5$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$3+\\sqrt2$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$2+2\\sqrt3$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$\\sqrt{17}$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "E",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Unfold by reflections",
        "body": "The path has four straight pieces: $A\\to BC\\to AD\\to BC\\to D$. Each piece crosses the square from one of the sides $AD$, $BC$ to the other, so it has a \"vertical\" part of length 1; the horizontal parts (along $AD$) add up to $AD=1$ if the path always moves towards $D$.\n\nReflect the square across $BC$, then the copy across its far side, and so on: the zigzag becomes a path from $A$ to the image of $D$, which is $4$ units away vertically and $1$ unit horizontally. The shortest path is the straight segment:\n$$\\sqrt{4^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{17}.$$\nIt touches $BC$ at horizontal positions $\\frac14$ and $\\frac34$ and $AD$ at $\\frac12$.\n\n**Answer: (E) $\\sqrt{17}$**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Cauchy–Schwarz",
        "body": "If the horizontal parts of the four pieces are $h_1,\\dots,h_4\\ge0$ (we may assume no backtracking) with $h_1+\\dots+h_4 = 1$, the length is $\\sum\\sqrt{1+h_i^2}$. By the Minkowski (triangle) inequality, $\\sum\\sqrt{1+h_i^2}\\ge\\sqrt{(1+1+1+1)^2+(\\sum h_i)^2}=\\sqrt{17}$, with equality when all $h_i=\\frac14$.\n\n**Answer: (E)**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "geometry"
    ],
    "difficulty": 2,
    "duplicateOf": null,
    "reviewNote": null
