    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "If $a<b<c$ are consecutive primes and the equation $ax^2+cx+b=0$ has an integer root, how many are the possible values of $b$?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "1"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "2"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "3"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "4"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "more than 4"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Rational root test",
        "body": "All coefficients are positive, so any root is negative. An integer root $r$ divides the constant term $b$ (from $b = -r(ar+c)$), so $r=-1$ or $r=-b$.\n\n- $r=-1$: $a-c+b=0$, i.e. $c=a+b$. If $a,b$ are both odd, $a+b$ is even and not prime; so $a=2$, $b=3$, $c=5$ — these are consecutive primes ✓ ($2x^2+5x+3=(2x+3)(x+1)$).\n- $r=-b$: $ab^2-cb+b=0$, i.e. $c=ab+1$. For odd $a,b$ this is even; for $a=2,b=3$ it gives $7\\ne5$.\n\nSo $b=3$ is the only possibility: **1** value.\n\n**Answer: (A) 1**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
