    "points": 5,
    "timeMinutes": null,
    "type": "mc",
    "prompt": "Three different numbers are chosen at random from the set $\\{1,2,3,\\ldots,10\\}$. What is the probability that one of them is the average of the other two?",
    "image": null,
    "choices": [
      {
        "key": "A",
        "text": "$\\frac1{10}$"
      },
      {
        "key": "B",
        "text": "$\\frac16$"
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "text": "$\\frac14$"
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "text": "$\\frac13$"
      },
      {
        "key": "E",
        "text": "$\\frac12$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "acceptedAnswers": null,
    "answerUnit": null,
    "solutions": [
      {
        "title": "Count by the outer pair",
        "body": "There are $\\binom{10}3=120$ equally likely choices. One number is the average of the other two exactly when the three form an arithmetic progression $x<m<z$. Such a triple is determined by the outer pair $x,z$, which must have the same parity (so that $m=\\frac{x+z}2$ is an integer). Pairs of same parity: $\\binom52+\\binom52=20$.\n\nProbability $\\frac{20}{120}=\\frac16$.\n\n**Answer: (B)**"
      },
      {
        "title": "Count by the middle",
        "body": "Middle number $m$ with common difference $d\\ge1$: need $m-d\\ge1$ and $m+d\\le10$, so $d\\le\\min(m-1,10-m)$. For $m=2,\\dots,9$: $1,2,3,4,4,3,2,1$ — total 20. Probability $\\frac{20}{120}=\\frac16$.\n\n**Answer: (B)**"
      }
    ],
    "topics": [
      "probability",
      "counting"
    ],
